      "id": 1,
      "text": "Newburgh was founded in 1829 by Abner Luce, just east of the former Sprinklesburgh settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newburgh%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After merging with the neighboring Luce-platted town in 1841, Newburgh blossomed into a bustling riverport by 1850.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.newburgh.in.gov/visitors/about_newburgh.php",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Newburgh served as a key Underground Railroad stop between the Little Pigeon River and Lake Michigan.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newburgh%2C_Indiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Angel Mounds, a National Historic Landmark about two miles west of town, preserves evidence of a Mississippian culture that thrived until 1450.",
